<div class="wp-shell">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <header class="wp-hero">
    <div class="wp-kicker">Proof of Work: How to Create Evidence of Your Skills &amp; Results </div>
    <h1>Create Evidence. Make Your Work Visible.</h1>
    <p>
      Learning is important, but learning alone does not always demonstrate
      what you can do. WorkProof helps you turn lessons, practice, projects,
      experiments, and completed work into evidence that another person can
      understand and evaluate.
    </p>
    <div class="wp-hero-actions">
      <button class="wp-btn wp-btn-primary" type="button"
        onclick="document.getElementById('wp-builder').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});">
        Create an Evidence Block
      </button>
      <button class="wp-btn wp-btn-outline" type="button"
        onclick="document.getElementById('wp-library').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'});">
        Explore Evidence Examples
      </button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- INTRO -->
  <section class="wp-section">
    <div class="wp-section-heading">
      <div class="wp-eyebrow">01 / THE PURPOSE</div>
      <h2>Shift from claiming ability to presenting evidence.</h2>
      <p>
        A course completion, a skill claim, and a demonstrated result are
        different things. WorkProof helps you connect what you learned to
        something observable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wp-grid">
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">01</div>
        <h3>Learning</h3>
        <p>
          Identify the concept, tool, method, or skill you are studying.
          Record what you understand and what you still need to practice.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">02</div>
        <h3>Application</h3>
        <p>
          Use the knowledge to solve a problem, complete an exercise,
          build something, or perform a task under defined conditions.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">03</div>
        <h3>Evidence</h3>
        <p>
          Preserve the result. Explain what you did, how you did it,
          what changed, and where someone can inspect the work.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="wp-callout">
      <strong>Core principle:</strong>
      Do not rely on a statement such as "I know JavaScript."
      Show the code, explain the function, demonstrate the result,
      and identify what you personally completed.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CLAIM VS EVIDENCE -->
  <section class="wp-section">
    <div class="wp-section-heading">
      <div class="wp-eyebrow">02 / CLAIM VS. PROOF</div>
      <h2>What makes a statement credible?</h2>
      <p>
        A claim describes what you believe you can do. Evidence gives
        another person something concrete to examine.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wp-compare">
      <div class="wp-compare-box bad">
        <h3>Claim without supporting evidence</h3>
        <div class="wp-quote">"I am good at website development."</div>
        <p>
          This does not identify the project, tools, responsibilities,
          technical decisions, or completed result.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="wp-compare-box good">
        <h3>Evidence-supported statement</h3>
        <div class="wp-quote">
          "I created a responsive resource directory using HTML, CSS,
          and JavaScript, including category filters and search."
        </div>
        <p>
          The statement identifies the work, tools, functionality,
          and a result that can be inspected.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EVIDENCE BLOCK FRAMEWORK -->
  <section class="wp-section">
    <div class="wp-section-heading">
      <div class="wp-eyebrow">03 / THE EVIDENCE BLOCK</div>
      <h2>Six components that make work understandable.</h2>
      <p>
        A useful evidence record should communicate more than a title
        and a screenshot. It should explain the work and its significance.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wp-grid">
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">01</div>
        <h3>Diagnostic Hook</h3>
        <p>
          What problem, need, question, or opportunity caused you
          to begin the work?
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">02</div>
        <h3>Artifact Core</h3>
        <p>
          What did you create, complete, analyze, repair, organize,
          or demonstrate?
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">03</div>
        <h3>Scalable Output</h3>
        <p>
          What can the work be used for? Can it be repeated, improved,
          extended, or adapted to another situation?
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">04</div>
        <h3>Supporting Evidence</h3>
        <p>
          What files, screenshots, links, records, test results,
          or demonstrations verify the work?
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">05</div>
        <h3>Business or Practical Impact</h3>
        <p>
          What problem does the work solve? What time, effort,
          uncertainty, or cost could it reduce?
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="wp-card">
        <div class="wp-number">06</div>
        <h3>Reflection</h3>
        <p>
          What did you learn? What limitations remain?
          What would you improve in the next version?
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EVIDENCE BUILDER -->
  <section class="wp-section" id="wp-builder">
    <div class="wp-section-heading">
      <div class="wp-eyebrow">04 / INTERACTIVE WORKSPACE</div>
